INT. MUNICIPAL SWIMMING POOL - DRAINED - DAY

An Olympic-length basin, empty to the tiles. Lane lines still hang from their reels, slack as old rope. A faded blue fish is painted at the deep end. Pigeons walk the gutter.

HOLLIS BRANDVOLD (60s, lanky, a lifeguard whistle still on a cord around his neck) stands on the pool floor with a push broom. He sweeps nothing in particular.

NADIA BRANDVOLD (30s, his daughter, city-council lanyard, work shoes ill-suited to wet tile) climbs down the steel ladder one rung at a time. At the bottom she stops and looks up at the ceiling lights.

He keeps sweeping. She walks toward him.

HOLLIS
Careful by the drain. It's slick.

NADIA
It's been closed since March.

HOLLIS
Still slick.

She reaches him. He doesn't stop. She takes hold of the broom handle. He lets her. For a moment they both hold it, neither moving.

NADIA
They vote Thursday.

HOLLIS
I heard.

NADIA
I'm going to vote yes.

He nods at the painted fish. His thumb rubs the whistle.

HOLLIS
Your mother used to swim here at six a.m. Every day. Forty laps.

NADIA
I know.

HOLLIS
She hated the cold water. She did it anyway.

NADIA
Dad.

He takes the broom back. She lets go. He sets it against the wall and they both look at the deep end, where the tile is cracked in a long, clean line.

HOLLIS
Fill it with dirt. Plant something.

NADIA
That's the plan.

He nods. She stands beside him. Neither leaves. A pigeon lifts out of the gutter and crosses the empty basin, and both of them follow it with their eyes until it clears the rim.
